WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:05.470
Narrator: For years,
they've been called illegal.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:08.053
Now the government says
they are essential.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.290
That's the confusing reality

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.310
for at least half of the
2.4 million farmworkers

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.270
who plant and harvest our food

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.775
here in the United States.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.940
Many produce pickers

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.340
are working under grueling conditions

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.270
with few safety measures in place.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.035
And that was already a problem

00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:38.840
even before hundreds began

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.767
testing positive for coronavirus

00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:43.325
in farms across the country.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.720
After 13 years working

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.020
in the fields of Southern California,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.570
Carmelita still does not

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.900
have legal status in the US.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.060
And because of that,

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.010
she cannot even think about signing up

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.480
for basic benefits,

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.050
like health insurance,

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.410
and she doesn't qualify
for a stimulus check

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.370
from the federal government.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.500
Overcoming these kinds of challenges

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.723
isn't anything new for Carmelita.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.000
What's different is

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.417
that now she's terrified of getting sick.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.170
By mid-May, a vast
majority of farm laborers

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.690
around the country say

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.320
they had still not received

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.680
masks, gloves, or any
other protective equipment

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.453
from the companies they work for.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.403
Carmelita had been taking
her own precautions.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:51.810
But many of her coworkers

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.570
didn't believe or understand the outbreak,

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.403
so keeping social distance
has been difficult.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.190
With peak harvesting
season already underway,

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:22.847
farmworker advocates fear

00:02:22.847 --> 00:02:25.610
that negligence and lack of education

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.450
could lead to a major outbreak.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.450
That, in turn,

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:30.730
could further disrupt

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.130
an already fragile supply chain

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.240
and leave empty shelves in grocery stores.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.720
- We're treated as
essential workers right now

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.930
because if we don't do this kind of work,

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.800
the United States is not going to have

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.148
food in supermarkets,

00:02:48.148 --> 00:02:51.003
food to feed the nation.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.530
Narrator: One doctor from the same

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.330
county Carmelita lives in

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:57.330
has been trying to help.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.110
Tipu Khan has been going from farm to farm

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.700
educating workers about the virus.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:06.720
- They live in our community,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.380
they function in our society.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.070
So we want to get in to them

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:11.340
and really help them be safe.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.220
Just trying to provide

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.330
basic medical education on how

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.610
to keep the infrastructure
of our society safe,

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.493
to keep the rest of society safe.

00:03:19.493 --> 00:03:21.700
Narrator: Not all farms have welcomed him,

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:23.920
but he says some are taking steps

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.423
to protect their workers.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:28.760
GoodFarms, which hires domestic workers

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.070
and international workers
with temporary work permits,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.440
is putting hand-wash
stations in the fields.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.660
- Part of the work that
we're having to do is

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.970
to not only look at the work,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.030
but also interface with our teams in both

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.007
their personal lives

00:03:46.007 --> 00:03:47.823
and the lives of their families.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:51.910
Narrator: California leads the nation

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.520
in growing produce

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.325
and is home to 400,000 farmworkers,

00:03:56.325 --> 00:04:00.940
of which an estimated
50% are unauthorized.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.780
In mid-April, California
Governor Gavin Newsom

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.130
announced a one-time
COVID-19 relief payment

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:10.257
of $500 per adult to approximately

00:04:10.257 --> 00:04:14.220
150,000 unauthorized immigrants.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.760
He also said that farms

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.720
and other food industry businesses

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.560
must give workers an additional two weeks

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.403
of COVID-19 sick leave.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.180
That would make a huge
difference for Carmelita,

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.540
who says her company normally only gives

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.277
three sick days per year.

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:36.990
More sick leave right now

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.440
would help with the current situation,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.890
but this kind of work can be backbreaking

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.120
at the best of times, say advocates.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.970
And the challenges for America's

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.960
undocumented farmworkers,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.623
and ultimately its food supply, remain.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.150
When Carmelita finishes her

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.690
12-hour shifts picking strawberries,

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.600
she heads to her two children

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.433
at a rented room in a mobile home.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:17.480
She dreams of the security
that legal status would bring.

00:05:28.371 --> 00:05:30.990
But the options for applying are limited,

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.480
and it can be very expensive.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.570
The pandemic has only made it harder.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.990
President Donald Trump
has used the outbreak

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.560
to suspend most legal immigration.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.420
Temporary guest worker programs

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.750
have continued to protect the food supply.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:47.930
But those programs only cover

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:51.140
10% of farmworkers in the US.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.420
And it doesn't help Carmelita,

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.000
since she's been here without legal status

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.251
for more than a decade.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.260
The "blue card" is part of a law

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:24.260
proposed by Democrats in 2017

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.180
that would give agricultural workers

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.700
the right to work,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.330
and a path to permanent legal status

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.223
for them and their families.

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.570
Carmelita's sons were born in the US,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.070
making them American citizens.

00:07:07.157 --> 00:07:10.824
Still, she feels proud
of the work she does.

